---
import ToolPage from '@versal/ui/ToolPage.astro';

const title = "JSON Escape & Unescape";
const description = "Escape plain text into a valid JSON string literal, decode an escaped literal back to readable text, and get a JavaScript-source-safe variant. No upload.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How do I escape JSON special characters?",
    "answer": "Paste plain text into the input and click Escape. The tool converts it to a properly escaped JSON string literal (double quotes, backslashes, newlines, tabs, Unicode). Click Unescape to reverse the process and decode an escaped string back to plain text."
  },
  {
    "question": "Why do I need to escape JSON?",
    "answer": "When embedding JSON strings inside other JSON documents, or inserting text into JSON configuration files, special characters must be escaped. Unescaped quotes or newlines break JSON validity. This tool handles all required escapes including Unicode."
  }
];
import ToolNav from '@versal/ui/ToolNav.astro';
import BaseLayout from '@versal/ui/BaseLayout';
---

<ToolPage
  site="main"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://jsonversal.com/tools/json-escape/"
  current="/tools/json-escape/"
  faqs={faqs}
  about={[
    {
      "heading": "What the two directions actually do",
      "body": [
        "Escape mode is a single `JSON.stringify` call on the raw input, so the output is always a complete, quoted JSON string literal rather than a bare fragment. Only what JSON requires is escaped: the double quote, the backslash, and the C0 control range. Tabs become `\\t`, newlines become `\\n`, and any other character below U+0020 becomes a `\\u00XX` escape. Ordinary non-ASCII text is passed through untouched rather than being escaped to `\\u` form.",
        "Unescape mode is the inverse `JSON.parse`. If the trimmed input starts with a double quote it is parsed as-is; otherwise quotes are wrapped around it first, so you can paste `He said \"hi\"` without supplying the delimiters. A malformed escape produces a parse error instead of a partial result, which is the behaviour you want when auditing log output or a corrupted config value."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The JavaScript-source variant and byte counts",
      "body": [
        "The second output row additionally escapes U+2028 and U+2029. Both are legal inside a JSON string, but historically rejected by JavaScript parsers in string-literal position, so output pasted into source, a shell argument, or an inline script can break on engines that predate the JSON-superset change. The primary row deliberately leaves them alone, since escaping them would misrepresent the JSON.",
        "Byte counts are measured with `TextEncoder`, so they report UTF-8 rather than JavaScript string length. That distinction matters for non-ASCII input: a CJK character is one length unit but three bytes, and an emoji outside the basic plane is two units and four bytes. The input and output figures together tell you exactly what the escaping cost."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Where escaping goes wrong in practice",
      "body": [
        "The common failure is wrong scope rather than wrong characters. Escaping yields a `\"…\"` string literal, not a JSON document, so pasting the output where a document is expected gives you a quoted scalar instead of the object you intended. To embed a value into an existing JSON file, escape only the string fragment and assemble the surrounding punctuation, commas and braces yourself.",
        "Note also that the forward slash is never escaped, because JSON does not require it. The `</script>` sequence that matters when embedding JSON inside an HTML script block therefore passes through verbatim — escape it by hand if that is your target, and remember that an unescaped newline inside a JSON string is what turns a valid config into a parse error."
      ]
    }
  ]}
>

  <section class="card mt-2">
    <h2 class="h2-card">Input</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <label class="text-muted" for="mode">Mode</label>
      <label for="mode" class="sr-only">Input</label> <select id="mode">
        <option value="escape" selected>Escape JSON</option>
        <option value="unescape">Unescape JSON</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <label for="input" class="sr-only">Input</label>
    <textarea id="input" class="mt-1" spellcheck="false" placeholder="Paste text to escape, or an escaped JSON string literal to unescape."></textarea>
    <div class="row mt-1">
      <button class="primary" id="run">Run</button>
      <button class="ghost" id="copy">Copy</button>
      <button class="ghost" id="sample">Load sample</button>
      <button class="ghost" id="clear">Clear</button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card mt-2" id="out-section" style="display:none;">
    <div class="row" style="margin-bottom:12px;">
      <h2 class="h2-flat" id="out-title">Output</h2>
      <div class="row" style="gap:8px;">
        <span class="text-dim" id="stats" style="font-size:.85rem;"></span>
        <button class="ghost" id="copy-out">Copy</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre id="output" class="mt-1">—</pre>
    <div id="js-row" class="mt-2" style="display:none;">
      <div class="row" style="margin-bottom:8px;">
        <h3 style="margin:0;">For JavaScript source</h3>
        <button class="ghost" id="copy-js">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre id="out-js" class="mt-1">—</pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card mt-2" id="err-section" style="display:none;">
    <h2 class="h2-error">Invalid escaped string</h2>
    <pre id="err-out" class="mt-1" style="color:var(--danger);">—</pre>
  </section>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Escape</strong> serializes the input with JSON.stringify: quotes, backslashes, newlines, tabs and control characters become the short \u-escapes that JSON requires, wrapped in double quotes.</li>
    <li><strong>Unescape</strong> parses the literal back with JSON.parse, restoring the original text  — handy for decoding API logs, CSV dumps, or generated payloads.</li>
    <li>The <strong>For JavaScript source</strong> row shows the same literal with U+2028 and U+2029 also escaped, so the string is safe to paste into older JS engines that reject those characters in source code.</li>
    <li>Byte counts (UTF-8) for input and output are shown under the result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>About JSON Escape & Unescape</h2>
  <p class="text-muted">Escaping is the price of putting arbitrary text inside a JSON document: quotes and backslashes must be marked, and control characters cannot appear literally. Getting the escaping wrong produces invalid JSON  — a leading cause of broken configs, failed API calls, and corrupted import scripts.</p>
  <p class="text-muted">This tool removes the guesswork. Escape raw text before embedding it in a config file, a request body, or a test fixture; unescape when you pull an escaped literal out of logs and want to read it. Both directions run locally, so sensitive payloads never leave your browser.</p>
</ToolPage>

<style>
  .row { display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }
  textarea { min-height: 200px; }
  pre { min-height: 60px; max-height: 240px; overflow: auto; }
  #output { white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-word; }
  #out-js { white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-word; font-size: .85rem; }
</style>

<script is:inline>
(function () {
  const $ = id => document.getElementById(id);

  function flash(btn, label) {
    const original = btn.textContent;
    btn.textContent = 'Copied!';
    setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = original; }, 1200);
  }

  function run() {
    const input = $('input').value;
    if (!input) { showToast('Please enter some text first.'); return; }
    const mode = $('mode').value;
    const enc = new TextEncoder();
    try {
      if (mode === 'escape') {
        const lit = JSON.stringify(input);
        const jsLit = lit.replace(/\u2028/g, '\\u2028').replace(/\u2029/g, '\\u2029');
        $('output').textContent = lit;
        $('out-js').textContent = jsLit;
        $('js-row').style.display = 'block';
        $('out-title').textContent = 'Escaped JSON string literal';
      } else {
        const t = input.trim();
        const text = t.startsWith('"') ? JSON.parse(t) : JSON.parse('"' + input + '"');
        $('output').textContent = text;
        $('js-row').style.display = 'none';
        $('out-title').textContent = 'Decoded text';
      }
      $('stats').textContent = 'Input ' + enc.encode(input).length + ' bytes → Output ' + enc.encode($('output').textContent).length + ' bytes';
      $('out-section').style.display = 'block';
      $('err-section').style.display = 'none';
    } catch (e) {
      $('err-out').textContent = 'Invalid escaped string: ' + (e.message || String(e));
      $('err-section').style.display = 'block';
      $('out-section').style.display = 'none';
    }
  }

  $('run').addEventListener('click', run);
  $('mode').addEventListener('change', () => { if ($('input').value) run(); });

  $('copy').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText($('input').value); flash($('copy'), 'Copy'); } catch {}
  });
  $('copy-out').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText($('output').textContent); flash($('copy-out'), 'Copy'); } catch {}
  });
  $('copy-js').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText($('out-js').textContent); flash($('copy-js'), 'Copy'); } catch {}
  });

  $('sample').addEventListener('click', () => {
    if ($('mode').value === 'escape') {
      $('input').value = [
        'He said "hello" and left.',
        'Path: C:\\Users\\bob\\docs\\report.txt',
        'Line 1\t(tab here)',
        'Unicode: 你好 🌍',
      ].join('\n');
    } else {
      $('input').value = 'He said \\"hello\\".\\nPath: C:\\\\Users\\\\bob\\\\docs\\nUnicode: \\u4f60\\u597d \\ud83c\\udf0d';
    }
    run();
  });

  $('clear').addEventListener('click', () => {
    $('input').value = '';
    $('out-section').style.display = 'none';
    $('err-section').style.display = 'none';
  });
})();
</script>
